   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to the recycling of electronic equipment",
    "basePrintNo": "S6089",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6089-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§27-2601, 27-2603, 27-2605 & 27-2617, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6089\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to the\nrecycling of electronic equipment\n\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill makes technical corrections to Title 26 of Article 27 of the\nEnvironmental Conservation Law and authorizes manufacturers to assess\na fee for the collection of electronic waste from businesses.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nThe following provisions are added to Title 26 of Article 27 of the\nEnvironmental Conservation Law \"Electronic Equipment Recycling and\nReuse\" as proposed in legislative bill numbers S.6047 and A.9049:\n\nParagraph 1 of §27-2603 is amended by adding a new subparagraph b to\nclarify the acceptance responsibilities of manufacturers.\n\nParagraphs 4 and 8 of §27-2605 are amended to authorize manufacturers\nto assess a fee for the collection of electronic waste from consumers\nhaving full time employees.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThis bill makes technical corrections to Title 26 of Article 27 of the\nEnvironmental Conservation Law and will allow manufacturers to\ncontinue the current practice of including the take-back of end of\nterm electronic equipment when contracting with businesses. The\namendment seeks to allow the continuation of the contractual\narrangement currently agreed to between manufacturers and businesses.\nIt is common for manufacturers and electronic waste recyclers to offer\nrecycling services to commercial entities. This practice has been\nbeneficial to the capture of electronic waste and should be continued\nunder the new statewide e-waste recycling program.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the same date and in the same manner as\na chapter of the laws of 2009 amending the environmental conservation\nlaw relating to the recycling, reuse, and safe handling of electronic\nequipment sold in the state of New York, as proposed in legislative\nbills numbers S.6047 and A.9049, takes effect.\n",
